d "360239d4-c271-52f3-8d25-ed47e7dd52f7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068433 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $a=-2,$ $b=5,$ and $c=-1.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(5)^{2}-4(-2)(-1) \\\\  & =25-8 \\\\  & =17\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068434 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10611" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068434 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $-x^2-x+1=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068434 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "f667bd43-407c-55a9-9821-67a0c1b11a96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068434 #edb/ref 1074 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-1,$ $b=-1,$ and $c=1,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-1)^{2}-4(-1)(1) \\\\  & =1+4 \\\\  & =5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is positive $(\\mathcal{D}>0),$ the equation has two distinct solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068435 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10612" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068435 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $x^2+2x+k=0$ has no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068435 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "5d4530b7-c6df-583f-b98f-8a8f93203295" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068435 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation has no real solutions if its discriminant is negative. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is negative.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(k) \\\\  & =4-4k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is negative:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & <0 \\\\ 4-4k & <0 \\\\ 4 & <4k \\\\ 1 & <k \\\\ k & >1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have no real solutions if $k>1.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068436 #edb/ref 1069 "e-10616" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068436 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation $x^2+2x -2 = 0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068436 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b92b9a39-e29a-532f-ba2c-f935773d0bf7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068436 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=1,$ $b=2,$ and $c=-2.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(2)^{2}-4(1)(-2) \\\\  & =4+8 \\\\  & =12\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $12$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true. First, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050465 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069513 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069524 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233154 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532647 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532678 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068832 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "87636457-bdd5-53dd-9137-c070a4e12e84" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069417 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069525 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233155 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233156 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234446 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234447 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1039 "Using the Discriminant to Determine the Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068833 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "926a6ba8-ec05-568a-9c13-c9f4d096e89f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049986 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069007 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069350 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233301 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234054 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186234056 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532625 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1039 "Choosing a Parameter to Force a Desired Number of Solutions to a Quadratic Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068834 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "452d826b-4cb3-56e1-989a-b025b5d5c955" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049935 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050358 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069069 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186069070 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233532 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186233533 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532642 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186532709 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1039 "Identifying Quadratic Equations With Rational Solutions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186068436 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068835 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "adc75098-5640-5e81-a0bc-003fa20c090c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186069775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1045 "The Discriminant of a Quadratic Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186069770 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1050 660.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186068958 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "94c7fd02-3d1a-53d9-a362-f4b944c65591" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069770 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069771 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069770 #edb/ref 1061 40122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069770 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4cec28d-0479-591e-8c9b-bc2853d4190f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069770 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069996 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069771 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069772 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069771 #edb/ref 1061 40125 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069771 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "14778f39-1f57-525b-ae31-35dcbc061af2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069771 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068832 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069772 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069773 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069772 #edb/ref 1061 40128 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069772 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "975d250d-99af-536a-8f81-90af7bf4ecad" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069772 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068833 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069773 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069774 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069773 #edb/ref 1061 40131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069773 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "6a7f97a5-c2ac-50f8-9a3f-b4d8062db921" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069773 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068834 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069774 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186069775 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069774 #edb/ref 1061 40147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069774 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2f5a9238-3e51-5658-bd57-7ad3172c3e89" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069774 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186069997 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069775 #edb/ref 1061 40148 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069775 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "9c9562e4-85a3-56b8-b173-0a8cfad0356a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069775 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186068835 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069996 #edb/ref 1064 "Given a quadratic equation in the form \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0,\n$$\n\nwe can find its roots using the quadratic formula:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}\n$$\n\nIn this formula, the expression under the square root is called the **discriminant**:\n\n$$\n\\mathcal{D} = {\\color{blue}b^2 - 4ac}\n$$\n\nThe value of the discriminant $\\mathcal{D}$ tells us a lot about the solutions of our quadratic equation.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}>0,$ then we get $2$ distinct real solutions: one with the positive square root and one with the negative square root.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}=0,$ then we get $1$ real solution because the square root vanishes. We sometimes say that the equation has two equal solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}<0,$ then we get no real solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples:\n\n- If $x^2+3x+2=0,$ then we have a positive discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=3^2-4(1)(2) = 1$ and $2$ distinct real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{3^2-4(1)(2)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm \\sqrt{1} }{2} = \\dfrac{-3 \\pm 1}{2} = -2,-1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+2x+1=0,$ then we have a zero discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=2^2-4(1)(1) = 0$ and $1$ distinct real solution:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{2^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm \\sqrt{0} }{2} = \\dfrac{-2 \\pm 0}{2} = -1\n$$\n\n- If $x^2+x+1=0,$ then we have a negative discriminant $\\mathcal{D}=1^2-4(1)(1) = -3$ and no real solutions:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{1^2-4(1)(1)} }{2(1)} = \\dfrac{-1 \\pm \\sqrt{-3} }{2}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069996 #edb/ref 1065 6630 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069996 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "79f4f718-3e60-5bd3-8e3b-25b092e91c76" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069996 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069997 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we have the quadratic equation \n\n$$\nax^2+bx+c=0\n$$\n\nwhere \n\n- the coefficients $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are all *rational numbers,* and\n\n- the discriminant is non-negative, i.e., $\\mathcal D \\geq 0.$\n\nThen, we have the following possibilities:\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is a *perfect square*, then the equation has two *rational* solutions.\n\n- If $\\mathcal{D}$ is *not* a perfect square, then the equation has two *irrational* solutions.\n\nLet's illustrate with some examples.\n\nConsider the equation $x^2-x-6=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-6) \\\\  & =1+24 \\\\  & =25 \\\\  & ={5}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a perfect square. Consequently, the corresponding solutions are both rational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{5^2}}{2(1)} = 3, -2\n$$\n\nNow, consider the equation $x^2-x-5=0.$ Its discriminant is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & =(-1)^{2}-4(1)(-5) \\\\  & =1+20 \\\\  & =21,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is *not* a perfect square. Consequently, the solutions are both irrational numbers:\n\n$$\nx = \\dfrac{1 \\pm \\sqrt{21}}{2}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069997 #edb/ref 1065 6633 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069997 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1fd92ab0-4173-58f8-8b37-79f116479768" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186069997 #edb/ref 1067 "Rational Solutions of Quadratic Equations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532497 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1069 "q-10318" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $-15{x}^{2}+kx-60=0$  has one distinct real solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "24a9b789-abfb-5cbd-9e43-cbbe69e980a1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233071 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation has one distinct real solution if its discriminant is zero. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is zero.\n\nFirst, we calculate the discriminant. We have $a=-15,$ $b=k,$ and $c=-60,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(k)^{2}-4(-15)(-60) \\\\  & ={k}^{2}-3\\,600\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is zero:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D=0 \\\\ {k}^{2}-3600 & =0 \\\\ {k}^{2} & =3600 \\\\ k & =\\pm \\sqrt{3600} \\\\ k & =\\pm 60\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have one distinct real solution if $k=\\pm 60.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532535 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112849" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}-12x+9=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "96eb4e16-d3f8-5bec-99cb-02da24948e7d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186233156 #edb/ref 1074 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=4,$ $b=-12,$ and $c=9,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-12)^{2}-4(4)(9) \\\\  & =144-144 \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is zero $(D=0),$ the equation has one distinct real solution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531938 #edb/ref 1080 "120" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531938 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531938 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "020f7ee6-efe4-5f13-82e0-365ef9f43144" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531955 #edb/ref 1080 "k>6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531955 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531955 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0bc16e37-ecee-5679-a64a-218fee94f795" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531959 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531959 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531959 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0d9f5221-4063-5d13-89d0-9cbeae6dfa3d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531960 #edb/ref 1080 "II only" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531960 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531960 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0e90531f-10e7-5a64-a37d-2ff16fbe4573" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186531969 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 175921860454