[17592186532535 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532535 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532329 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532535 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5d18037b-3674-5480-8ce7-b35031e52aae" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532009 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532356 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532448 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532228 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532548 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "78b0844e-f95a-518b-84f1-546d37ff9d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531959 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532047 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532484 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532319 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532610 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f2d3e179-11b4-52f5-bce3-6a401512952d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531955 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186531982 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532121 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186532336 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186532331 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532613 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "f9816bbe-93b2-52b6-837d-9867d4d1aa47" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532613 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1069 "q-44577" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1071 "Find the values of $k$ for which the quadratic equation $3{x}^{2}+12x+k=0$ has two distinct real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "10d98fee-4a02-5f93-813f-e8d53425cb38" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532625 #edb/ref 1074 "The equation has two distinct real solutions if its discriminant is positive. So, we need to find the values of $k$ so that the discriminant is positive.\n\nWe have $a=3,$ $b=12,$ and $c=k,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={12}^{2}-4(3)(k) \\\\  & =144-12k.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we find the values of $k$ such that the discriminant is positive:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & >0 \\\\ 144-12k & >0 \\\\ 144 & >12k \\\\ k & <12.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the given equation will have two distinct real solutions if $k<12.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532610 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158003" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation $4{x}^{2}+6x+1=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "389f1f33-a04d-5409-a2d5-8e337f759108" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532642 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=4,$ $b=6,$ and $c=1.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(6)^{2}-4(4)(1) \\\\  & =36-16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532548 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112843" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045486 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $4(x-2)^{2}-8=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "41b9a165-df20-54af-99cb-dea16d3c7585" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532647 #edb/ref 1074 "First, note that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4(x-2)^{2}-8 & =4({x}^{2}-4x+4)-8 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-16x+16-8 \\\\  & =4{x}^{2}-16x+8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe have $a=4,$ $b=-16,$ and $c=8.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-16)^{2}-4(4)(8) \\\\  & =256-128 \\\\  & =128\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532503 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112846" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the equation $-2{x}^{2}+6x-5=0.$ How many distinct real solutions does it have?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "79550be8-05ba-52d9-a59a-1c06a72e87c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532667 #edb/ref 1074 "To quickly determine the number of solutions, we can compute the discriminant. We have $a=-2,$ $b=6,$ and $c=-5,$ so the discriminant is as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & ={6}^{2}-4(-2)(-5) \\\\  & =36-40 \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the discriminant is negative $(D<0),$ the equation has no real solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532517 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1069 "q-112838" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1071 "Calculate the discriminant of the quadratic equation $5{x}^{2}+6=0.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8b3abfcf-5108-5885-9c88-671c529d21e5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532678 #edb/ref 1074 "We have $a=5,$ $b=0,$ and $c=6.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(0)^{2}-4(5)(6) \\\\  & =0-120 \\\\  & =-120\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186532500 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1069 "q-158008" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the quadratic equation $2{x}^{2}-2x-2=0.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The discriminant of the equation is a perfect square.\n\nII. The equation has only rational solutions.\n\nIII. The equation has only irrational solutions." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d9f98f1e-4801-5a10-97a0-297033319c24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186532709 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. We have $a=2,$ $b=-2,$ and $c=-2.$ So, we calculate the discriminant as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}D & ={b}^{2}-4ac \\\\  & =(-2)^{2}-4(2)(-2) \\\\  & =4+16 \\\\  & =20\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAnd $20$ is *not* a perfect square.\n\n- Statement II is false, while statement III is true.\n\nFirst, notice that our quadratic equation has rational coefficients. And, since the discriminant is not a perfect square, the equation must have two irrational solutions.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
